#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:3
msgid "WatchDog Module Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:5
msgid ""
"This file provides information on the module parameters of many of the Linux "
"watchdog drivers.  Watchdog driver parameter specs should be listed here "
"unless the driver has its own driver-specific information file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:10
msgid ""
"See Documentation/admin-guide/kernel-parameters.rst for information on "
"providing kernel parameters for builtin drivers versus loadable modules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:16
msgid "watchdog core:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:17
msgid "handle_boot_enabled:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:18
msgid ""
"Watchdog core auto-updates boot-enabled watchdogs before userspace takes "
"over. Default is set by the kconfig option "
"CONFIG_WATCHDOG_HANDLE_BOOT_ENABLED."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:22
msgid "open_timeout:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:23
msgid ""
"Maximum time, in seconds, for which the watchdog framework will take care of "
"pinging a running hardware watchdog until userspace opens the corresponding /"
"dev/watchdogN device. A value of 0 means an infinite timeout. Setting this "
"to a non-zero value can be useful to ensure that either userspace comes up "
"properly, or the board gets reset and allows fallback logic in the "
"bootloader to try something else. Default is set by the kconfig option "
"CONFIG_WATCHDOG_OPEN_TIMEOUT."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:31
msgid "stop_on_reboot:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../watchdog/watchdog-parameters.rst:32
msgid "Stops watchdogs on reboot (0 = keep watching, 1 = stop)."
msgstr ""

